program            = 001_complex_declarators
area               = 10_declarations_and_types
description        = Complex declarators: arrays of pointers to functions returning pointers, pointer to array, function returning a function pointer, folded and runtime variants
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
Every call is through a fully initialized function pointer: table and direct_table are
static arrays initialized with the addresses of functions of exactly the matching type,
and typedef_slot is assigned pick_slot directly. pick_slot indexes slot_table with i & 1,
so the index is always 0 or 1 and can never leave the two-element array. row_ptr points at
grid[1], a genuine element of grid, and only elements 0 through 2 of that row are read, all
of which are initialized. No pointer is ever formed one past the end and dereferenced. All
arithmetic is on small int values far from the int range edges, so there is no signed
overflow: the largest intermediate value is 27. No shift is performed. No object is
modified twice between sequence points, and no printf call receives more than one
side-effecting argument, so unspecified evaluation order cannot affect the output. No
storage is read before it is written. There is no aliasing violation: every object is
accessed only through an lvalue of its own declared type. No padding byte is read.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
Both printed sizes are of int-based arrays only: sizeof grid is a 3x3 int array (36 bytes)
and sizeof slot_table is a 2-element int array (8 bytes). Both are therefore identical on
all four targets, because int is 4 bytes on x86_64, i686, aarch64 and riscv64 alike. No
sizeof of long, pointer, double, long long or any aggregate containing them is printed, so
the measured i686 difference (4-byte long and pointer versus 8 bytes elsewhere) cannot
surface. No address or pointer value is printed anywhere: pointer facts appear only as
dereferenced int values. Plain char is not used, so the measured char signedness difference
(signed on x86_64 and i686, unsigned on aarch64 and riscv64) is irrelevant here. The only
printf conversion specifier used in the program is %d.
END
expected_stdout    <<END
fn_table_folded=27
fn_table_runtime=21
slot_values=11 22
direct_table=11 22
row_ptr=4 5 6
choose=11 10
sizes=36 8
END
